  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="wire" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.1 · a verified local connection</span>
        <h2>The request cannot claim its own permission.</h2>
        <p><code>dekopon-run</code> opens a fresh local connection for each request. It checks the private socket and expected broker user, sends one size-limited message, reads one size-limited response, and closes the connection.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="authority-state-machine">
        <div><span>the agent can send</span><strong>request fields</strong><small>capability · JSON input · request ID · trace IDs</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">private local socket →</i>
        <div class="authority-broker-box">
          <span>operating system + your config</span>
          <div><strong>local user</strong><b>→</b><strong>trusted identity</strong><b>→</b><strong>permission rule</strong></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→ clear result</i>
        <div><span>the agent receives</span><strong>result + safe evidence</strong><small>never a reusable permission</small></div>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">The agent can send</th><th scope="col">The agent cannot send</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>protocol version and one strict message shape</td><td>its own local or agent identity</td></tr>
            <tr><td>unique request and durable trace IDs</td><td>an allow/deny decision or rule revision</td></tr>
            <tr><td>capability name and size-limited untrusted JSON</td><td>approved HTTP hosts, methods, or runtime limits</td></tr>
            <tr><td>an optional valid W3C <code>traceParent</code></td><td>credentials or the broker’s one-use approval</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Socket mode <code>0600</code> creates one local-user trust domain. Every process under that user can use its configured agent identity. Use a dedicated operating-system user when that separation matters. This is a local safeguard, not per-process proof or multi-tenant identity.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="lifecycle" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.2 · one request from start to finish</span>
        <h2>Nine checks keep the action and record together.</h2>
      </div>

      <ol class="mental-model-list">
        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Verify the connection.</strong><p>The broker gets the local user from the connection and looks up one trusted identity. Unknown users see no capability list.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Read under hard limits.</strong><p>Dekopon checks the message length before allocating memory, applies one deadline, and rejects unknown JSON fields.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Reserve the request ID.</strong><p>An ID found in verified audit history or accepted by the running broker cannot be reused, even after a denial.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Load trusted tool details.</strong><p>The broker—not the model or provider—chooses the capability and provider details used by the permission rule.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Check the exact rule.</strong><p>The local identity, agent, capability, provider, read/write behavior, retry safety, and limits must all match. Otherwise the answer is no.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>06</span><div><strong>Record the decision.</strong><p>Every valid request from a known local user gets an audit decision before provider work begins.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>07</span><div><strong>Create one-use approval.</strong><p>A private <code>AuthorizedInvocation</code> ties the request, decision, provider, and limits together inside the broker. It cannot be copied or returned.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>08</span><div><strong>Run in a fresh sandbox.</strong><p>The host applies the approved HTTP limits and collects safe request evidence that provider code cannot widen or hide.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>09</span><div><strong>Record the result.</strong><p>The broker ties the outcome, evidence, decision, request, and trace together, then updates its local audit checkpoint.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="policy" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.3 · fine-grained permissions</span>
        <h2>Every layer can only narrow access.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="data-journey-map">
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-remote">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>untrusted</span><strong>agent request</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node"><small>asks for</small><strong><code>jsonplaceholder.posts.create</code></strong><code>cannot claim an identity</code></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ exact permission rule</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-host">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>trusted broker code</span><strong>one-use approval</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>identity</small><strong>local user + agent</strong><code>from the connection map</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">+</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>operation</small><strong>capability + provider</strong><code>write · not safe to retry</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">+</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>limits</small><strong>exact HTTP access</strong><code>host · POST · 1 call</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ used once, never returned</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-guest">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>limited execution</span><strong>provider + Dekopon host</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>provider</small><strong>build request</strong><code>cannot set credential headers</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ check →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>network client</small><strong>pin + send</strong><code>no proxy · no redirect</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ record →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>evidence</small><strong>safe metadata</strong><code>no body or URL path</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Rust types make it harder to create or reuse approval by accident. The stronger protection comes from layers working together: a separate broker, verified local connection, trusted identity map, exact rule, one-use approval, narrow host access, and durable record order.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="audit" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.4 · durable audit</span>
        <h2>The audit record is part of the action.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="run-mode-cards">
        <article class="run-mode-current">
          <div>{{ status("current", "Per record") }}<span>owner-only JSONL</span></div>
          <h3>Hash-linked records</h3>
          <p>The broker opens one owner-only file, refuses symlinks, takes the only writer lock, verifies existing records, and syncs every decision and result.</p>
          <ul><li>partial records stop startup</li><li>changed content or hash links fail verification</li><li>accepted request IDs rebuild replay protection</li></ul>
        </article>
        <article class="run-mode-future">
          <div>{{ status("current", "Per append") }}<span>separate local file</span></div>
          <h3>Separate local checkpoint</h3>
          <p>After syncing the audit file, the broker safely replaces a second file that keeps the expected record count and final hash.</p>
          <ul><li>startup requires the checkpoint to match verified history</li><li>one audit record written just before a crash can recover</li><li>larger gaps or rollback stop the broker</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>The checkpoint detects an audit file that was shortened while the checkpoint stayed in place. It does not make the machine tamper-proof. Someone who can roll back or delete both files can defeat the local comparison. Signed or remote checkpoint copies come next.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="failures" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.5 · safe retries</span>
        <h2>The error tells you whether work may have happened.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Code / outcome</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Retry posture</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>unauthenticated</code></th><td>Connected UID has no trusted mapping.</td><td>Do not retry until mapping changes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>invalid-request</code></th><td>The size-limited request did not decode.</td><td>Correct it; use a fresh request ID.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>broker-unavailable</code></th><td>No provider work began.</td><td>Safe to resubmit with a fresh request ID.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>outcome-unaudited</code></th><td>Provider work may have completed but terminal audit failed.</td><td><strong>Do not resubmit.</strong> Reconcile manually.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Denied result</th><td>The decision was recorded; the provider did not run.</td><td>Change the rule or request—not the ID.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Failed result</th><td>An approved provider attempt failed and was audited.</td><td>Check whether the action can write or safely retry before sending a new request.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The client never retries on its own. If a response to a non-repeatable write is lost, the result is unknown. Creating a new request ID and trying again would bypass replay protection and could repeat the action.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.6 · where trust ends</span>
        <h2>Strong local safeguards. Not multi-tenant production yet.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "Shipped") }}</div><h3>Local permission broker</h3><p>Private socket setup, exact local-user mapping, limited concurrency, clean shutdown, limited providers, audit, checkpoint recovery, and OpenTelemetry spans.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("direction", "Next") }}</div><h3>Broker-managed credentials</h3><p>Version 0.2.0 does not inject provider secrets. Destination-bound credential handling needs its own permission and redaction tests.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("direction", "Next") }}</div><h3>Production hardening</h3><p>Remote audit anchors, non-Unix transport, per-process identity, tenancy, key management, and incident automation remain future work.</p></article>
      </div>

